From: gtk-gnutella project <https://github.com/Zero3K20/gtk-gnutella>
Date: 2026-03-25
Subject: [PATCH] [NTOSKRNL/MM] Fall back to top-down VAD search when bottom-up fails

When NtAllocateVirtualMemory is called with a NULL base address and
MEM_RESERVE (or MEM_RESERVE|MEM_COMMIT), MiInsertVadEx performs a
bottom-up scan of the process VAD tree via MiFindEmptyAddressRangeInTree.
If the bottom portion of the address space is heavily fragmented—for
example because many PE sections from GTK+/GLib and their dependencies
were mapped at non-pre-rebased addresses—this scan can find no suitable
contiguous gap and immediately returns STATUS_NO_MEMORY.

The cascade that follows is:
  1. NtAllocateVirtualMemory returns STATUS_NO_MEMORY.
  2. The VirtualAlloc caller receives NULL.
  3. Subsequent VirtualFree(NULL, ...) or VirtualFree of the NULL-
     derived pointer logs "Unable to find VAD for address 0x00000000".
  4. Stack-overflow guard-page extension also calls ZwAllocateVirtualMemory
     and fails → "Close to our death..." → MDL probe crash.

The fix: when the bottom-up scan fails (Result == TableFoundNode) and
the process is not already in top-down mode, automatically retry with
MiFindEmptyAddressRangeDownTree.  Top-down search starts from the
highest valid user address, which is typically far away from the DLL/heap
cluster at the bottom of the address space.  This mirrors the fallback
strategy that Windows NT applies internally for processes whose
bottom-of-space region is saturated.

The fallback only activates when the initial scan has already failed, so
it introduces no regression for normal allocations.

Affected file: ntoskrnl/mm/ARM3/vadnode.c

diff --git a/ntoskrnl/mm/ARM3/vadnode.c b/ntoskrnl/mm/ARM3/vadnode.c
--- a/ntoskrnl/mm/ARM3/vadnode.c
+++ b/ntoskrnl/mm/ARM3/vadnode.c
@@ -290,16 +290,30 @@ MiInsertVadEx(
         }
         else
         {
-            /* Find an address bottom-up */
+            /* Find an address bottom-up first */
             Result = MiFindEmptyAddressRangeInTree(ViewSize,
                                                    Alignment,
                                                    &CurrentProcess->VadRoot,
                                                    &Parent,
                                                    &StartingAddress);
+
+            /*
+             * If the bottom-up scan found no gap (e.g. because many DLLs
+             * were mapped at non-pre-rebased addresses and fragmented the
+             * lower address space), fall back to a top-down scan.  The top
+             * of the user address range is almost always clear of the heap /
+             * DLL cluster and can accommodate large reservations that would
+             * otherwise fail with STATUS_NO_MEMORY.  This matches the
+             * effective behaviour of Windows NT in heavily-fragmented
+             * address spaces.
+             */
+            if (Result == TableFoundNode)
+            {
+                Result = MiFindEmptyAddressRangeDownTree(ViewSize,
+                                                         HighestAddress,
+                                                         Alignment,
+                                                         &CurrentProcess->VadRoot,
+                                                         &StartingAddress,
+                                                         &Parent);
+            }
         }
 
         /* Get the ending address, which is the last piece we need for the VAD */
